  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nAn upper ridge is building into the Northern Plains with a \nsouthwest flow pattern. This will trend temperatures toward \nabove average, particularly by Sunday and Monday. Water vapor \nsatellite imagery shows a plume of moisture moving northward \ninto the region to allow for low level clouds to begin \noverspreading the area late tonight. The way this upper ridging \nbuilds with moisture transport will promote a pattern with low \nstratus, fog and drizzle - especially with a low sun angle. \nChances for drizzle and light showers peak Saturday afternoon \nand linger into the evening, especially south of US Highway 2. \nThe expected cloud cover continues to lower confidence in \nexactly how warm temperatures get over the next few days. With \nat least partial sun, temperatures could soar well into the 60s \non Sunday.\n\n...Rain Event Monday/Tuesday...\n\nGetting into early to mid next week, ensemble guidance is \nmostly in agreement on a persistent deformation band of rain as \nan upper shortwave trough in the Southwest translates into the \nCentral Plains. While the best forcing looks to remain south of \nour area, there could be enough influence from this feature to \nbring the next round of light to moderate rain into the \nforecast, heavily favoring the southern zones. A few ensemble \nmembers keep Grand Forks and points north mostly dry given a \nfurther south storm track. Monday-Tuesday Night (48 hour) \nprobabilities for at least a half inch of rainfall range from \naround 80% in Fergus Falls down to 40% for Langdon at this time.\nA lack of cold air behind the system results in near zero \nchance for frozen precipitation.",
